# Example configuration for tools/check_no_leaks.py
#
# Copy this file to `.leaks.local` in the repository root and replace the
# placeholder lines with the identifiers that must not appear.
#
# `.leaks.local` is untracked (see .gitignore section 1).  That is the point:
# the real list never enters the repository, so the scanner does not leak the
# identifier it is protecting.  This example file is tracked and therefore
# carries placeholders only.
#
# The scanner fails closed -- exit 2 -- if `.leaks.local` is missing.  That is
# deliberate.  A scan that reports success while unconfigured manufactures
# confidence rather than providing it.
#
# Format: two sections, one entry per line, blank lines and # comments ignored.
# Matching is case-insensitive, so one entry covers every casing.

[content]
# Strings that must not appear in any tracked file's text.
# Example placeholder -- replace with a real identifier.
REPLACE-WITH-A-FORBIDDEN-STRING

[path]
# Filename fragments that must not appear in any tracked path.
# Example placeholder -- replace with a real one.
REPLACE-WITH-A-FORBIDDEN-FILENAME-FRAGMENT

# Note on the trade-off in .gitignore section 2:
#
# A pattern written directly into .gitignore fires on every `git add`, with no
# dependency on anyone remembering to run a scanner.  Moving the name check
# into this file gives that up in exchange for not printing the identifier in
# a tracked pattern.
#
# Whichever way you resolve it, resolve it once and write the reason down.  A
# half-enforced rule is read as enforced.
